WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.280
- Hey, I'm Daniel Kaluuya

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:03.630
and this is how I became
Chairman Fred Hampton.

00:00:05.248 --> 00:00:06.670
(soft music)

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:07.940
- Deputy Chairman Fred Hampton

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:08.773
of the Illinois Black Panther Party.

00:00:08.773 --> 00:00:12.230
- Well, it means that the young Patriots,

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:13.970
you know what the Confederate flag?

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:15.052
That scene?

00:00:15.052 --> 00:00:17.210
That was the first day
and I was really nervous.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:19.281
And I think it was something
that wasn't working,

00:00:19.281 --> 00:00:20.350
the scene wasn't working and then

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:22.758
we managed to figure out
why it wasn't working.

00:00:22.758 --> 00:00:25.180
I think it was the first
time I'd done the voice

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:29.410
and had everything in
a scene, like halfway

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.800
through the scene and
then it was just like.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.010
We'll be okay, 'cause I think I tapped,

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:41.010
I think that day was me
shedding the iconness of him.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:44.701
And I actually was really embodying him.

00:00:44.701 --> 00:00:49.500
And, but I had to just kind
of wear it for like half a day

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:54.500
in order to feel it and
then go, oh yeah, this fits!

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.120
Let's go.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.874
You know what I'm saying?

00:00:58.874 --> 00:01:00.220
And, and so that's for
me, it was like halfway

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:02.200
through the first day when I realized,

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.430
I was like, oh I'm playing a man.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:10.430
Who has a goal and loves
this group of people.

00:01:10.605 --> 00:01:12.219
- [Man] I will learn all I can.

00:01:12.219 --> 00:01:13.823
[Audience] I will learn all I can.

00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:15.202
- [Man] In order to give my best to my

00:01:15.202 --> 00:01:16.800
people in the struggle for liberation.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.010
- It was a tipping point when,

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:20.550
when I went to Chicago,
when I went by myself

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.670
to Chicago for like a week,
that was the tipping point.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.050
I wouldn't call it more
important than anything else

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.284
'cause I think everything is as important

00:01:26.284 --> 00:01:28.730
even as the small details.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:32.027
But it was a tipping
point in terms of like,

00:01:32.027 --> 00:01:33.814
"Cool I feel I'm in here,"

00:01:33.814 --> 00:01:34.647
do you understand?

00:01:34.647 --> 00:01:36.130
But I think it was just
like, I needed to get

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.880
into that space and just
completely focus on it

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.493
for a week in order to
understand like, yeah,

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:47.060
I can hit, I can sit in a certain position

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.940
and be a vessel that I'm aspiring to be.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.001
- [Man] You can murder a freedom fighter,

00:01:52.001 --> 00:01:53.780
but you can't murder freedom!

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:54.860
- Usually in other processes

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:56.463
I would have a collection of people,

00:01:56.463 --> 00:01:58.470
that I was like, this is the area

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.560
that I would like a bit
of this and a bit of that.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.450
So I would just listen to people

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:04.820
that I felt like I wanted
to have a vibe of this.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:06.350
I want to have this vibe and I want to go

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:07.900
into that direction.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.250
And with this, I looked for
people that sounded similar

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.729
to Chairman Fred, and
just there wasn't much.

00:02:13.729 --> 00:02:18.729
He's got a very distinct way of speaking.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.130
And I just had to focus on him, you know?

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:25.250
But, yeah, what that
gifted me was a new way

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:26.710
of approaching things was that,

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:29.040
Oh, I need to really get into-

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.000
if speech is a window into how you think-

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.070
I need to get into his thinking

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:36.880
in order to move in the
space of his speech.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.285
And so I was like, Oh, okay cool.

00:02:38.285 --> 00:02:41.770
Like, why does this river make sense?

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:42.700
Why am I saying this word?

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:47.410
How are the new thoughts igniting

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:49.630
from the thought before, you know?

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:53.010
Igniting in a different way,
which is why you're speaking

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.260
in that way which is why
it's like you see that

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:56.700
in the classroom scene.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.890
For me it was like
"tidididiba tidididiba."

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:01.850
He just goes "doosh," he goes "doosh."

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:03.520
But he can take his time.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:04.730
But then the turn is "Bow!"

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:05.680
Do you understand?

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.463
And that's what's quite thrilling
about the way he speaks.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.200
I saw that from watching him.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.460
It's like, Oh, it's like
this, this, this, this, this

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:18.660
and boom!

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:19.493
This, this, this, this,
this, this, then boom!

00:03:19.493 --> 00:03:20.717
Another direction.

00:03:20.717 --> 00:03:21.820
This, this, this, this, then boom!

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:26.189
And I'm like, no one
can even see that turn.

00:03:26.189 --> 00:03:31.020
So I need to process those thoughts

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.760
in that ways, in order
to replicate the river.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.890
- Imagine what we could
accomplish together.

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:37.140
We can heal this whole city.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.437
- I think that's the
alarming thing I found

00:03:40.437 --> 00:03:41.400
about Chairman Fred.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.440
There was no comparison.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.500
There was no touchstone
that I could kind of go,

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.740
Oh, I get it.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:47.850
It was like, when I read the script,

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:49.448
I said this is brand new.

00:03:49.448 --> 00:03:52.180
This is like I have never seen.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:53.750
What the hell is happening here?

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:58.415
I remember Shaka had
two touchstones that he

00:03:58.415 --> 00:04:00.980
kind of thought of that helped me

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:03.470
access the voice actually,
which was Bernie Mac,

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.420
and then he mentioned Richard Pryor.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:07.430
It's not something that
I had internalized,

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:10.900
but he was just saying
the command that they had

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:15.180
over an audience was similar.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.550
And I think they're both Illinois.

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:18.669
Yeah, they're both from Chicago.

00:04:18.669 --> 00:04:21.230
And Richard Pryor was definitely that era,

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:26.180
so I know I found old-school
Richard Pryor tapes.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.150
And I was listening to that to find

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:31.680
if there was anything that
would feed what was building.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.020
When I was in Chicago, I figured out

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.410
I don't want any specific ones,
but the ones from 1968, 69

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.024
were like, you can't find them.

00:04:39.024 --> 00:04:40.470
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:44.457
So I know I want that,
but then it didn't feed me

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:46.213
in that way.

00:04:46.213 --> 00:04:48.790
But, I think knowing that he listened

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:51.273
to Malcolm X and listened
to Martin Luther King

00:04:51.273 --> 00:04:54.250
in the movie and in his regular day

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:57.127
in order to build his oratorial abilities

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:01.840
gave me a window into
his thinking even more.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.140
But I wouldn't compare him

00:05:04.668 --> 00:05:06.310
to Martin Luther King or Malcolm X.

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:09.970
He kind of absorbed those ideas
and created something new.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:11.090
That's the genius of him.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:15.070
He absorbed all these ideas
and created something original.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:16.797
Like, original thoughts
in an original way.

00:05:16.797 --> 00:05:17.630
You know what I'm saying?

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:21.455
That it hasn't been seen again

00:05:21.455 --> 00:05:25.708
in the public domain,
you know what I'm saying?

00:05:25.708 --> 00:05:27.289
That was probably the biggest mountain.

00:05:27.289 --> 00:05:30.163
It was like, I had no
touchstone, it was no one.

00:05:30.163 --> 00:05:31.661
I was like, Oh, this is like this.

00:05:31.661 --> 00:05:32.807
And this is like that.

00:05:32.807 --> 00:05:34.164
I've said before like
the widow's character,

00:05:34.164 --> 00:05:35.873
I saw it as a beady seagull.

00:05:35.873 --> 00:05:36.714
You know what I'm saying?

00:05:36.714 --> 00:05:37.547
So I was like, "boom!"

00:05:37.547 --> 00:05:38.380
All right, cool.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:39.213
I see, I know that.

00:05:39.213 --> 00:05:40.046
I know that way.

00:05:40.046 --> 00:05:40.879
I know a couple of guys, I grew up with,

00:05:40.879 --> 00:05:41.712
you know what I'm saying?

00:05:41.712 --> 00:05:43.456
And so like I knew the world.

00:05:43.456 --> 00:05:45.330
I knew the mentality in that sense.

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:49.250
I knew the decision-making,
this is incomparable.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:50.497
- [Man] I am

00:05:50.497 --> 00:05:51.519
- [Audience] I am

00:05:51.519 --> 00:05:52.361
- [Man] A revolutionary!

00:05:52.361 --> 00:05:53.239
- [Audience] A revolutionary!

00:05:53.239 --> 00:05:54.072
- [Man] I am

00:05:54.072 --> 00:05:54.929
- [Audience] I am

00:05:54.929 --> 00:05:55.762
- [Man] A revolutionary!

00:05:55.762 --> 00:05:56.595
- [Audience] A revolutionary!

00:05:56.595 --> 00:05:57.428
- [Man] I am

00:05:57.428 --> 00:05:58.261
- [Audience] I am

00:05:58.261 --> 00:05:59.094
- [Man] A revolutionary!

00:05:59.094 --> 00:05:59.927
(drumming music)

00:05:59.927 --> 00:06:00.760
- [Audience] A revolutionary!

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.590
- I think it was Shaka, Lucas brothers'

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:06.680
and Will Burson's decision
to make it a genre piece.

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:11.000
Thus making it accessible
for prime viewers

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:13.185
and thriller watches.

00:06:13.185 --> 00:06:15.300
And, you know, in that sense,

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:16.730
cause it's kind of like
a similar narrative

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:20.545
to Donnie Brasco, or The
Departed, or you know

00:06:20.545 --> 00:06:25.545
those kinds of films and so in that sense

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:27.410
if someone loves that sort of narrative

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:28.430
they're going to be drawn upon it

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:32.713
and then meet this
world and these politics

00:06:32.713 --> 00:06:34.618
and these views and these perspectives

00:06:34.618 --> 00:06:38.096
and Chairman Fred Hampton, you know?

00:06:38.096 --> 00:06:42.850
So that, for me, is what separates it, is

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:47.676
that as a genre piece, I
mean, a genre as opposed to

00:06:47.676 --> 00:06:52.540
just about it being about educating

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:57.540
and informing about the Black
Panther Party, it's like,

00:06:57.896 --> 00:07:00.073
what did Ryan and Shaka say?

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:02.816
You put the medicine in the food.

00:07:02.816 --> 00:07:04.340
Is how they saw it.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:05.740
- [Man] When I dedicated
my life to people,

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:07.171
I dedicated my life.

00:07:07.171 --> 00:07:09.810
- I think it was the 50
year anniversary that day.

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:11.560
We did a poison in that scene.

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:12.504
And then the scene where

00:07:12.504 --> 00:07:15.630
Chairman Fred makes a certain decision.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:20.630
And I found that day
personally really awful.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:24.919
To find the truth, get out of my own way.

00:07:24.919 --> 00:07:26.995
My emotions were flaring up.

00:07:26.995 --> 00:07:27.930
You understand?

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:29.805
So, I wasn't clear.

00:07:29.805 --> 00:07:34.330
I realized how entangled
and meshed I was to this.

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:36.360
And I really felt some real things.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:41.360
And it was kind of like stopping
me from accessing the truth

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:43.604
and the way I usually access it.

00:07:43.604 --> 00:07:46.510
And so it was more of that.

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:50.010
It was that I found
that really challenging

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:53.633
to find the way in the way
that I usually find it.

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:58.193
- [Man] Anywhere there's
people, there's power.

00:07:59.478 --> 00:08:02.890
- I think Shaka said it today, he said

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:06.143
what O'Neil isn't shows
you what Chairman Fred is.

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:10.073
I think it was, he says
it way more articulate

00:08:10.073 --> 00:08:12.340
than that, I just said it.

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:13.660
I'm going to text him after and ask him

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:14.970
for a specific quote.

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:17.620
But, yeah, so I think it's that.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:19.952
It's like you can't appreciate
the light without the dark.

00:08:19.952 --> 00:08:21.330
That's just life.

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:22.330
That's just truth.

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:23.490
That's law.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:26.150
So I think we were both in
service of Chairman Fred

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:30.070
is the reality, you know,
and then I've heard the Keef

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:34.330
articulate that for himself
in order to access what's

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:35.923
in places that he had to go to.

00:08:36.824 --> 00:08:41.824
So, yeah, I think we just
kind of sat in that truth

00:08:42.241 --> 00:08:45.750
and supported each other on
days that we needed support

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:48.700
when certain days where
he had to do some really

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:49.533
intense stuff.

00:08:49.533 --> 00:08:50.730
I was there, yo what'd you need?

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:53.020
And one of those days where
I needed to do intense stuff

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:55.420
he was there like, yeah,
yeah, what do you need?

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:56.690
So it kind of moved like that.

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:59.520
- The Black Panthers are
forming a Rainbow Coalition

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.613
of oppressed brothers and
sisters of every color.

00:09:04.207 --> 00:09:06.790
(steady music)

